    t,
    'en',
    pathname,
    slug,
    title,
    description,
    'Filing Relief for Natural Disasters Act',
    faq,
  )}
>
  <TopNav pageMode ctaHref={ctaHref} current="coverage" />
  <main>
    <section class="m-section m-page-hero" id="hero">
      <nav class="la-bc" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={hubHref} class="m-page-back">
          <span class="m-page-back__arrow" aria-hidden="true">←</span>IRS disaster relief
        </a>
        <span class="la-chip">P.L. 119-29 · enacted Jul 24, 2025</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="m-eyebrow">Legal background</p>
      <h1 class="m-page-title">
        Filing Relief for Natural Disasters Act: state-declared disasters can now move federal
        deadlines
      </h1>
      <p class="m-page-lead la-answer">
        Public Law 119-29 (H.R. 517, enacted July 24, 2025) amends IRC §7508A in two ways: the IRS
        may now postpone federal tax deadlines for a <em>state-declared</em> disaster at the
        governor’s written request, after consulting FEMA — and the mandatory automatic
        postponement under §7508A(e) grew from 60 to 120 days.
      </p>
      <p class="m-page-reviewed">
        Verified against congress.gov / govinfo as of <time datetime={reviewedOn}>{reviewedOn}</time>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="la-what-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="la-what-h">What the Act actually says</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        The official title is “To amend the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 to modify the rules for
        postponing certain deadlines by reason of disaster.” The operative change adds authority
        for the Treasury Secretary, “upon written request of the Governor of a State” and after
        consultation with FEMA, to apply §7508A’s postponement rules to a qualified state-declared
        disaster. Before this law, §7508A relief was keyed to federally declared disasters — a
        governor’s declaration alone moved nothing.
      </p>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        The second, quieter change: the minimum mandatory postponement period in §7508A(e) — the
        automatic runway taxpayers get in a declared disaster — doubled from 60 days to 120 days.
      </p>
      <p class="la-sources">
        <span class="la-sources__k">Sources</span>
        <a class="la-link" href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/517" rel="noopener" target="_blank">H.R. 517 — congress.gov ↗</a> ·
        <a class="la-link" href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-119publ29/html/PLAW-119publ29.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">P.L. 119-29 statute text — govinfo ↗</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="la-means-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="la-means-h">What it changes in practice</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        The trigger set for federal disaster relief got wider, not automatic. A governor’s
        declaration plus a written request can now produce an IRS postponement without a FEMA
        major-disaster declaration — useful for localized events that never reach the federal
        threshold. But the mechanism is permissive: the IRS still decides, and relief still
        arrives the same way it always has — as an IRS newsroom release with a relief code, a
        covered area, and a new date. The monitoring workflow does not change; the set of events
        worth watching does.
      </p>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        Note the asymmetry this creates with state conformity: the IRS can now follow a
        state-declared disaster — while many states still do not follow federally declared ones.
        Whether a given state matches any federal postponement remains a per-state question; see
        the <a class="la-link" href="/irs-disaster-relief/state-conformity">verified state
        conformity pages</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="la-companion-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="la-companion-h">The 2025 companion laws</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        Two other enacted laws complete the picture. P.L. 119-21 (signed July 4, 2025) made the
        qualified-disaster casualty-loss treatment permanent and — beginning in 2026 — extends the
        deduction to losses from state-declared disasters, per the IRS. And P.L. 119-64 (signed
        December 26, 2025) fixed the refund-lookback interaction of disaster postponements — see
        the <a class="la-link" href="/irs-disaster-relief/disaster-related-extension-of-deadlines-act"
        >Disaster Related Extension of Deadlines Act page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="la-sources">
        <span class="la-sources__k">Sources</span>
        <a class="la-link" href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/casualty-loss-deduction-expanded-and-made-permanent" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS — casualty loss deduction expanded and made permanent ↗</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block">
      <FaqList eyebrow="FAQ" title="P.L. 119-29 questions" items={faq} />
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block">
      <div class="m-page-cta">
        <div>
          <h2 class="m-page-cta__title">More triggers means more changes to catch</h2>
          <p class="m-page-cta__body">
            State-declared disasters can now move federal deadlines — one more stream of changes to
            watch. DueDateHQ monitors IRS, state, and FEMA sources and matches each postponement to
            the clients it affects, with the source on every date.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-page-cta__btns">
          <a class="m-btn m-btn--primary" href={ctaHref} data-event="marketing.legislation.cta"
            >Start free</a
          >
          <a class="m-btn m-btn--secondary" href={hubHref}>Current disaster relief</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

